From c34cbfd4b2b583c7236189b2e64224bac5dfe516 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: W1nkelm4nn <mrmarkap61@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 19:49:55 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] kk: Write pipeline archives without holding back pipeline
 builds

Writing an archive empties the capture of the compiler's serializer and
crashes Metal when that compiler builds a pipeline meanwhile, so builds
waited for every write: on a first start of Rise of the Tomb Raider the
game's compile threads waited 194 s in all for 27 writes. Another
compiler may build while one is written, though.

So pipelines build with one of two compilers, each with a serializer
and a lock of its own. A write switches the builds to the other
compiler, waits only for the builds still in progress with the first and
writes its capture; builds that still found the first one look again
once they have its lock. The write every 1,024 pipelines runs on the
queue of the writes instead of the thread that built the pipeline, and
the write when the device goes waits for one in progress, which may
have taken the other capture only. MESA_KK_COMPILE_STATS also prints
how long builds waited for writes.

Rise of the Tomb Raider filling its archives from scratch, one run
each: builds waited 0.0 instead of 194 s for 26 writes, mean 21.5
instead of 20.1 fps, Geothermal Valley 10.5 instead of 7.5 fps. Started
again, it took all pipelines from the archives but the 675 it had built
after the last write, before the test killed it.

